WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:07.102
A decision depends on costs that change between alternatives, not every cost assigned to a program.

2
00:00:07.102 --> 00:00:13.437
Marginal and incremental analysis separates relevant future differences from totals that will remain the same.

3
00:00:13.437 --> 00:00:24.839
This episode adapts Kioko and Marlowe's chapter Cost Analysis, published by University of Washington Pressbooks under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 except where otherwise noted.

4
00:00:24.839 --> 00:00:29.526
It condenses, paraphrases, and reorganizes the authors' explanatory prose.

5
00:00:29.526 --> 00:00:35.860
Separately credited cases, examples, figures, tables, quotations, and linked materials are excluded.

6
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:41.771
Variable costs move with activity, while fixed costs remain stable within a relevant range and time period.

7
00:00:41.771 --> 00:00:46.024
Step costs remain fixed until demand crosses a capacity threshold, then jump.

8
00:00:46.024 --> 00:00:52.543
Over a longer horizon, costs that looked fixed may become adjustable through staffing, leases, or capital replacement.

9
00:00:52.543 --> 00:00:55.429
Avoidable costs disappear if an activity stops.

10
00:00:55.429 --> 00:01:00.034
Sunk costs have already been incurred and should not drive the next choice.

11
00:01:00.034 --> 00:01:07.341
Opportunity cost represents the value of the best alternative use of constrained staff time, space, cash, or equipment.

12
00:01:07.341 --> 00:01:11.517
These categories are decision-specific rather than permanent labels.

13
00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:14.677
Build scenarios around service units and capacity.

14
00:01:14.677 --> 00:01:19.100
Separate volume from price, identify bottlenecks, and test thresholds.

15
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:30.285
An outsourcing proposal should include contract management, transition, quality control, retained overhead, and risk, not merely compare a vendor quote with allocated full cost.

16
00:01:30.285 --> 00:01:32.022
Avoid the average-cost trap.

17
00:01:32.022 --> 00:01:41.263
Dividing total annual cost by annual units can describe history, but it may not predict the cost of one more unit or the savings from one fewer unit.

18
00:01:41.263 --> 00:01:46.907
Show the relevant range, time horizon, capacity threshold, and resources that truly change.

19
00:01:46.907 --> 00:01:52.179
Include implementation delay and reversal cost when an option is difficult to unwind.

20
00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:54.032
Torrance application segment.

21
00:01:54.032 --> 00:02:10.711
This original local exercise is separate from the textbook and cites the City of Torrance Budget Documents page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six, at torrance C A dot gov slash Government slash Departments Offices slash Finance slash Budget Documents.

22
00:02:10.711 --> 00:02:14.038
Suppose a hypothetical City service faces higher demand.

23
00:02:14.038 --> 00:02:21.582
Begin with the current adopted budget and latest review records only to identify authorized resources and the reporting period.

24
00:02:21.582 --> 00:02:25.503
Model a base case, added-volume case, and capacity-threshold case.

25
00:02:25.503 --> 00:02:30.730
Separate overtime, materials, contracted capacity, supervision, facilities, and capital.

26
00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:35.483
Record which costs are avoidable, which remain, and when a step increase occurs.

27
00:02:35.483 --> 00:02:43.027
This exercise does not claim that any Torrance service has excess demand or recommend staffing, contracting, or capital action.

28
00:02:43.027 --> 00:02:47.222
The result should show both financial and operational consequences.

29
00:02:47.222 --> 00:02:54.172
A cheaper option that shifts waits, errors, safety risk, or unpaid work to residents is not equivalent service.

30
00:02:54.172 --> 00:03:01.060
Marginal analysis is strongest when it states what changes, what does not, and who experiences the difference.

31
